.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_HASH 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_murmur3 ,
.Nm xft_murmur3_with_seed ,
.Nm xft_xxh3_64bits
.Nd non-cryptographic hash functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In hash.h
.Ft t_u128a
.Fn xft_murmur3 "const t_u8 *restrict mem" "t_size size"
.Ft t_u128a
.Fn xft_murmur3_with_seed "const t_u8 *restrict mem" "t_size seed" "t_size size"
.Ft t_u64a
.Fn xft_xxh3_64bits "t_buffer input" "t_u64a seed"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In hash.h
provides the two hash functions used by
.Xr xft_indexmap 3
and
.Xr xft_swissmap 3 :
a 128-bit Murmur3 and a 64-bit XXH3.
Neither is cryptographic and neither is keyed.
Both are
.Ql pure ,
allocate nothing, and may be called concurrently from any number of
threads.
.Ss Murmur3
.Fn xft_murmur3_with_seed
hashes the
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa mem
using
.Fa seed
as the initial state and returns the 128-bit result.
The input is consumed in 16-byte blocks; a trailing fragment of up to 15
bytes is mixed in by
.Fn xft_murmur3_tail .
The two 64-bit lanes start at
.Fa seed ,
are updated independently per block, and are folded into a single
.Vt t_u128a
after the final mix.
.Pp
.Fn xft_murmur3
is
.Fn xft_murmur3_with_seed
with
.Fa seed
fixed at
.Ql 0x9e3779b185ebca87 .
.Pp
Both functions are declared
.Ql nonnull
on their first argument and
.Ql pure ,
and read exactly
.Fa size
bytes from
.Fa mem .
.Ss XXH3
.Fn xft_xxh3_64bits
hashes the
.Fa input Ns .size
bytes at
.Fa input Ns .mem
with the seed
.Fa seed
and returns a 64-bit result.
The function dispatches by input length:
.Bl -tag -width "241 bytes and up"
.It 0 to 16 bytes
.Fn xft_xxh3_len_0to16 ,
with sub-cases for 0, 1 to 3, 4 to 8 and 9 to 16 bytes.
.It 17 to 128 bytes
.Fn xft_xxh3_len_17to128 ,
mixing 16-byte pairs taken from both ends of the buffer against the secret.
.It 129 to 240 bytes
.Fn xft_xxh3_len_129to240 ,
mixing each 16-byte chunk against a window of the secret.
.It 241 bytes and up
.Fn xft_xxh3_hashlong_64b ,
a stripe-accumulator loop over 512-bit lanes followed by a fold of the eight
accumulators.
.El
.Pp
The long path derives a custom secret by adding a
.Fa seed Ns -dependent
pattern to each 64-bit lane of the default 192-byte secret.
The default secret itself is the constant table published with the XXH3
reference implementation and is returned by
.Fn xft_xxh3_get_secret
as a
.Vt t_buffer
without allocating.
.Pp
XXH3 is the default hash of
.Xr xft_indexmap 3
and
.Xr xft_swissmap 3 ,
which call it with a seed of 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each function returns the hash.
None of them fail or set an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_u64	hash_key(const t_u8 *key, t_size len)
{
	return (xft_xxh3_64bits(xft_fatptr((t_u8 *)key, len), 0));
}

t_u128a	hash_key_128(const t_u8 *key, t_size len)
{
	return (xft_murmur3(key, len));
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_indexmap 3 ,
.Xr xft_swissmap 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
Neither function is suitable for adversarial input; an attacker who controls
the keys can force collisions in a table built on them.
Use a keyed cryptographic hash for anything exposed to untrusted data.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xxh3_64bits
does not check
.Fa input Ns .mem
for
.Dv nullptr ;
its internal length helpers reach
.Ql __builtin_unreachable
if it is, so
.Fa input Ns .mem
must be a valid pointer even for zero-length input.
.Pp
The seed of
.Fn xft_murmur3
is a compile-time constant.
Two builds of
.Nm
with the same source produce the same hashes; a different seed requires
calling
.Fn xft_murmur3_with_seed
directly.
